sections.body:
`@Component` is the generic, foundational stereotype annotation: 'this class should be scanned and registered as a bean.' `@Service`, `@Repository`, and `@Controller` are all, themselves, annotated with `@Component`, meaning they are specializations of it, and as far as *bean registration* goes, they behave identically to a plain `@Component`. So why bother distinguishing them at all?

First, semantic clarity: seeing `@Repository` on a class immediately tells a reader 'this talks to a data store,' and `@Service` says 'this holds business logic,' which is useful documentation that a bare `@Component` doesn't provide. Second, `@Repository` adds behavior: a `PersistenceExceptionTranslationPostProcessor` (Spring Boot autoconfigures one when its data-access support is on the classpath) wraps `@Repository`-annotated beans so that technology-specific exceptions (a JPA `PersistenceException`, for example) get translated into Spring's own unified, unchecked `DataAccessException` hierarchy, meaning your service layer can catch one consistent family of exceptions regardless of which persistence technology sits underneath. Third, Spring MVC looks for `@Controller` (or `@RestController`, which includes it) when it registers request mappings. Put `@GetMapping` methods on a plain `@Component` in Spring 6 or later and the bean is created, but its URLs return 404. Finally, some tooling and AOP pointcuts target `@Service` or `@Repository`, so the wrong stereotype can exclude a bean from behavior meant for it.

```java
@Repository
public class JpaOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    // ... persistence-specific exceptions thrown here get translated automatically
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repository;

    public OrderService(OrderRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}
```

