sections.body:
Each autoconfiguration class is guarded by one or more conditional annotations that decide, at startup, whether it activates. `@ConditionalOnClass(SomeClass.class)` checks whether a specific class is present on the classpath. `JacksonAutoConfiguration`, for instance, is guarded by a check for Jackson's `ObjectMapper` class, so it only activates at all if some starter (typically `spring-boot-starter-web` or `-json`) has put Jackson on the classpath. `@ConditionalOnProperty` gates activation on a specific configuration property being set (or set to a particular value) in `application.properties`/`.yml`.

The one that matters most for everyday development is `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`. Many of Spring Boot's default beans (the embedded Tomcat factory, the default `ObjectMapper`, and dozens of others) are annotated `@ConditionalOnMissingBean(TheType.class)`, meaning: 'only register this default if no bean of this type already exists in the context.' Define your own `@Bean` of that type anywhere in your own configuration and Boot's default backs off, with no ambiguous-bean error. The ordering works because autoconfiguration classes are processed after all your own bean definitions, so the condition can see what you registered.

```java
@Configuration
public class JacksonConfig {
    @Bean
    public ObjectMapper objectMapper() {
        ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
        mapper.registerModule(new JavaTimeModule());
        return mapper;
    }
}
```

Because this `ObjectMapper` bean now exists in the context, `JacksonAutoConfiguration`'s own `@ConditionalOnMissingBean(ObjectMapper.class)`-guarded default bean method does not run, and your mapper is the one controllers use for JSON. (Spring Boot 4 uses Jackson 3 by default: there the bean type to define is `JsonMapper` from `tools.jackson.databind.json`, and java.time support is built in.)

examples.code:
@Configuration
public class JacksonConfig {

    @Bean
    public ObjectMapper objectMapper() {
        ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
        mapper.registerModule(new JavaTimeModule());
        mapper.disable(SerializationFeature.WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS);
        return mapper;
    }
}

